  "Question": "The graph of \\(y=2x^2+4x+3\\) has its:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "lowest point at \\((-1,9)\\)",
    "B": "lowest point at \\((1,1)\\)",
    "C": "lowest point at \\((-1,1)\\)",
    "D": "highest point at \\((-1,9)\\)",
    "E": "highest point at \\((-1,1)\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Completing the square makes the least possible value visible. Since \\((x+1)^2=x^2+2x+1\\), rewrite the function as\n\\[\ny=2\\bigl((x+1)^2-1\\bigr)+3\n=2(x+1)^2+1.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(x\\), the square \\((x+1)^2\\) is nonnegative, so \\(y\\ge1\\). Equality holds exactly when \\(x+1=0\\), or \\(x=-1\\).\n\nThus the graph has its lowest point at \\((-1,1)\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
